Q: Object does not respond to mouse events A: <p>The issue here is that you are using <code>var</code> to declare your variables, which get hoisted to the top scope. See <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Hoisting" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hoisting</a> for details. Because of the for-loop each iteration overwrites the single <code>innerText</code> variable instance that was hoisted.</p>
<p>You can resolve the issue by using a lexical declaration, i.e. <code>let</code> or <code>const</code>. In this way each <code>swapText</code> and <code>innerText</code> declaration maintains its lexical scoping, i.e. within the for-loop scope.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function swapInnerText0() {
  const spans = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;span[data-swapinnertext]&quot;);

  for (let s = 0; s &lt; spans.length; s++) {
    const swapText = spans[s].dataset.swapinnertext;
    const innerText = spans[s].innerText;

    spans[s].onmouseover = function () {
      this.innerText = swapText;
    };
    spans[s].onmouseout = function () {
      this.innerText = innerText;
    };
    spans[s].onclick = function () {
      this.innerText = swapText;
      this.onmouseover = null;
      this.onmouseout = null;
      this.removeAttribute(&quot;data-swapinnertext&quot;);
    };
  }
}
</code></pre>
